<!-- ──────────────────────────── §01 THE MISMATCH -->
<section id="s-mismatch" data-code="mismatch">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>The draft model's hidden exposure bias</h2>
<p>
  Speculative decoding trains draft models on sequences generated by the target model — standard knowledge distillation with teacher forcing. EAGLE-3, DFlash, and SpecForge all follow this pattern: the draft is shown target-generated tokens as context at every position, and learns to predict the next target token. <span class="idea-tag existing">existing</span>
</p>
<p>
  The mismatch appears at inference. The draft doesn't see target-generated tokens as context. It sees a <em>mixture</em>: tokens that were accepted (a biased sample from the joint draft-target distribution), interspersed with tokens that were rejected and resampled from the target. This mixture is never encountered during off-policy training.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">draft-mismatch.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Press Play — watch how inference context differs from training context</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="canvas-wrap">
      <canvas id="mm-canvas" height="215"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-btns">
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="mm-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="mm-reset">↺ Reset</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Every current paper on spec decoding training acknowledges some form of this mismatch. HASS (arXiv:2408.15766) calls it "context inconsistency" and proposes a supervised fix. CORAL (arXiv:2502.16880) addresses representation drift across multi-step training. But neither changes the fundamental question: <em>what distribution should the draft's training sequences be sampled from?</em>
</p>
<div class="callout">
  <strong>The on-policy distillation literature already answered this for language models:</strong> the student should generate its own training sequences. The same answer applies to draft models — but no paper has made this connection and built the necessary infrastructure. The closest is DistillSpec (arXiv:2310.08461, 2023), which noted the on/off-policy distinction for spec decoding KD but did not use acceptance/rejection signals as training signal.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ──────────────────────────── §02 ACCEPTED DISTRIBUTION -->
<section id="s-accepted" data-code="accepted">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Mathematics</div>
<h2>What the accepted-token distribution actually looks like</h2>
<p>
  Standard speculative decoding uses the following acceptance criterion: token <em>x</em> proposed by the draft is accepted with probability min(1, π<sub>T</sub>(<em>x</em>) / π<sub>D</sub>(<em>x</em>)). When rejected, a token is resampled from the residual distribution max(0, π<sub>T</sub> − π<sub>D</sub>) / Z. This determines exactly what tokens end up in the context at inference time.
</p>

<!-- Acceptance distribution bar chart SVG -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">acceptance-distribution.svg</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:.875rem">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 640 200" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace">
      <!-- Axes -->
      <line x1="52" y1="10" x2="52" y2="155" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,.12)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <line x1="52" y1="155" x2="630" y2="155" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,.12)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <!-- Y-axis label -->
      <text x="8" y="83" font-size="7.5" fill="#64748b" transform="rotate(-90,8,83)" text-anchor="middle">P(token)</text>
      <!-- Gridline at 0.5 -->
      <line x1="52" y1="82" x2="630" y2="82" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,.04)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
      <text x="46" y="85" font-size="6.5" fill="#475569" text-anchor="end">0.5</text>
      <text x="46" y="157" font-size="6.5" fill="#475569" text-anchor="end">0.0</text>
      <!-- Legend -->
      <rect x="460" y="8" width="10" height="8" rx="2" fill="rgba(148,163,184,.55)"/>
      <text x="473" y="16" font-size="7" fill="#94a3b8">Target π_T</text>
      <rect x="460" y="22" width="10" height="8" rx="2" fill="rgba(139,92,246,.55)"/>
      <text x="473" y="30" font-size="7" fill="#a78bfa">Draft π_D</text>
      <rect x="460" y="36" width="10" height="8" rx="2" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.55)"/>
      <text x="473" y="44" font-size="7" fill="#10b981">Accepted min(π_D,π_T)</text>
      <rect x="460" y="50" width="10" height="8" rx="2" fill="rgba(245,158,11,.55)"/>
      <text x="473" y="58" font-size="7" fill="#f59e0b">Resampled max(0,π_T-π_D)</text>
      <!-- Token labels -->
      <!-- 6 tokens: "Paris","sunny","warm","is","the","capital" -->
      <!-- Token 1: Paris — draft overestimates (rejected often) -->
      <!-- πT=0.35, πD=0.55 → accepted=0.35, resampled=0  -->
      <!-- Token 2: sunny — draft underestimates (always accepted) -->
      <!-- πT=0.25, πD=0.10 → accepted=0.10, resampled=0.15 -->
      <!-- Token 3: warm — draft underestimates -->
