<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Acceptance criterion (standard speculative decoding)</div>
  Accept token <span class="math-var">x</span> if <span class="math-var">rand() &lt; π_T(x) / π_D(x)</span>       <span class="math-comment">← accept probability = min(1, π_T/π_D)</span>
  Marginal accepted distribution: <span class="math-var">P_accepted(x) ∝ min(π_D(x), π_T(x))</span>
  Resampled tokens: <span class="math-var">P_resample(x) ∝ max(0, π_T(x) − π_D(x))</span>
  Full context = <span class="math-var">α × P_accepted + (1−α) × P_resample</span>   where <span class="math-var">α = Σ min(π_D, π_T)</span>
</div>

<p>
  The context distribution the draft sees at inference is therefore <em>not</em> π<sub>T</sub> (what off-policy training uses) and <em>not</em> π<sub>D</sub> (what on-policy sampling of the draft alone would give). It is a mixture dominated by tokens where π<sub>D</sub> ≈ π<sub>T</sub>, with overestimated tokens systematically absent (they were rejected and replaced). Training on this specific distribution is what "on-policy draft training" means.
</p>
<div class="callout-warn">
  <strong>Connection to MiniLLM:</strong> MiniLLM's core finding was that reverse KL (which samples from the student) outperforms forward KL (which samples from the teacher) precisely because the student's distribution is what matters at inference. The same argument applies here: the draft's context at inference comes from the <em>accepted-token distribution</em>, not from the target alone. Training on the target distribution (current practice) is the wrong reference point.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ──────────────────────────── §03 ON-POLICY DRAFT TRAINING -->
<section id="s-opd" data-code="opd">
<div class="section-no">03 — Idea A</div>
<h2>On-policy draft training through the accept/reject loop</h2>
<p>
  <span class="idea-tag proposed">proposed idea</span> The fix is to train the draft model on sequences produced by running the full speculative decoding loop — not on clean target sequences. The draft generates candidate tokens, the target accepts or rejects each one, and the training signal comes from both the acceptance pattern and the target's logits at accepted positions.
</p>
<p>
  This is structurally identical to GKD's on-policy training loop, with one addition: the acceptance/rejection mask provides a binary reward signal on top of the KL divergence objective. Tokens that are consistently rejected reveal that the draft is overestimating them — exactly the information that off-policy training never provides.
</p>

<h3>The 5-step on-policy training loop</h3>
<div class="step-list" id="opd-steps">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Draft generates K candidate tokens</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each training prompt, the draft model autoregressively generates K tokens. This is identical to inference — no teacher forcing. The draft's own hidden state is the context at each step.</div>
      <div class="step-code">draft_tokens = draft.generate(prompt, K=5, do_sample=True)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Target model verifies in one forward pass</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The full target model processes (prompt + draft_tokens) in a single parallel forward pass — the same tree-parallel verification used at inference time. Output: per-token acceptance mask + target logits.</div>
      <div class="step-code">mask, target_logits = target.verify(prompt, draft_tokens)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Compute acceptance-weighted on-policy loss</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Two loss components: (a) KL divergence from target logits at <em>accepted</em> positions — standard distillation signal. (b) REINFORCE penalty at <em>rejected</em> positions — the draft assigned too much probability to a token the target rejected, so penalise it proportionally to the rejection probability.</div>
      <div class="step-code">loss = kl_loss(accepted) + λ × reinforce_penalty(rejected)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Update draft weights</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Standard backward pass through the draft model only. The target model is frozen throughout. Gradient flows through both the KL and REINFORCE terms, pushing the draft's distribution toward the accepted-token distribution.</div>
      <div class="step-code">loss.backward(); optimizer.step()</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Updated draft runs next generation round</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The improved draft model generates the next batch of candidate tokens. The training distribution naturally shifts to match the new draft policy — this is the on-policy property. No static corpus accumulates distribution mismatch.</div>
      <div class="step-code">draft_v2.generate(prompt, K=5)  # distribution now shifted</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="opd-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span id="opd-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 5</span>
  <button id="opd-next">Next →</button>
</div>

<h3>Connection to existing work</h3>
<p>
  This proposal sits between several existing methods:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>DistillSpec</strong> (2310.08461): sampled from the draft for training data, but used forward KL loss only — no acceptance/rejection signal, no reject penalty</li>
  <li><strong>GTO</strong> (2509.22134): used a tree-level acceptance reward (PPO-style), but training was offline on batches, not interleaved with live generation</li>
  <li><strong>Aurora</strong> (2602.06932): used binary accept/reject as RL signal with async updates, but without a principled divergence objective and without proper RVT disaggregation</li>
  <li><strong>PPOW</strong> (2605.14978): window-level RL reward for acceptance length, offline training</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout-idea">
  <strong>What is genuinely new here:</strong> the combination of (a) on-policy generation from the current draft, (b) acceptance-weighted KL loss at accepted positions, and (c) REINFORCE penalty at rejected positions, derived from first principles from the accepted-token distribution derivation in §02. This creates a mathematically coherent objective that directly minimizes the TV distance between the draft and target distributions — the quantity that acceptance rate maximises.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ──────────────────────────── §04 DISAGGREGATED RVT -->
<section id="s-rvt" data-code="rvt">
<div class="section-no">04 — Idea B</div>
<h2>Disaggregated Rollout-Verify-Train</h2>
<p>
  <span class="idea-tag proposed">proposed idea</span> On-policy draft training requires running the full spec decode loop during training — which means paying the cost of both the draft model (rollout) and the target model (verify) in every training step. Naïvely, this doubles or triples the per-step cost vs. offline training. The fix is disaggregation: separate these three concerns into independent worker pools that scale differently.
</p>

