<h3>Why disaggregation is necessary</h3>
<p>
  The three phases have fundamentally different compute profiles:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rollout workers</strong> run the small draft model (1B–7B params) at high throughput. They are memory-bandwidth-bound, like decode workers in PD-disaggregated serving. Many are needed in parallel to generate enough training data. They hold only a frozen weight snapshot, no optimizer state.</li>
  <li><strong>Verify workers</strong> run the large target model (27B–70B params) for a single forward pass over the draft tree — this is structurally a prefill, not a decode. A PD-disaggregated system's prefill workers are already optimized for exactly this workload. The same hardware can serve both production requests and training verification batches.</li>
  <li><strong>Training workers</strong> need optimizer state (AdamW moments = 2× model parameters in fp32). This is the stateful, scarce resource. One or two of these can service many rollout workers running in parallel.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout-idea">
  <strong>The PD disaggregation insight</strong> is that speculative decoding verification is structurally identical to prompt prefill: a single matrix-multiply-heavy pass over the full sequence. This means verify requests can be routed to the prefill pool in a PD-disaggregated serving system — spec decoding training and production serving share the same hardware, amortising the cost of the large target model.
</div>

<h3>Comparison to Aurora</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Aurora</strong> (arXiv:2602.06932) is the closest prior work: an async training loop where accepted/rejected tokens become RL signals for a speculator model, with hot-swap weight updates between inference batches. It achieves 1.5x day-0 speedup and 1.25x additional gain under distribution shift. Aurora's limitations are exactly what RVT addresses:
</p>
<table class="results-table">
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Aurora</th><th>RVT (proposed)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Rollout scaling</td><td class="mn">1× inference server</td><td class="mn best">N× independent workers</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Verify scaling</td><td class="mn">co-located with training</td><td class="mn best">M× prefill workers (shared with serving)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Training</td><td class="mn">async, informal RL</td><td class="mn best">principled on-policy KL+REINFORCE</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weight consistency</td><td class="mn">ad-hoc async</td><td class="mn best">versioned snapshots, deterministic</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Throughput at scale</td><td class="mn">limited by single server</td><td class="mn best">scales with N rollout workers</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</section>

