  </div>
</div>

<h3>Theoretical prediction: why on-policy should help</h3>
<p>
  The acceptance rate for speculative decoding equals 1 − TV(π<sub>D</sub>, π<sub>T</sub>) averaged over positions. Minimising TV distance is exactly what the on-policy objective (KL at accepted positions + rejection penalty) is designed to do. Off-policy training minimises KL(π<sub>T</sub> ‖ π<sub>D</sub>) but on a distribution that doesn't match inference; the on-policy variant minimises the same KL but evaluated on the actual inference distribution. For K ≥ 2, each rejected token gives additional signal about where π<sub>D</sub> overestimates, tightening the TV bound more per training step.
</p>

<h3>Falsification conditions</h3>
<p>
  The ideas here are <em>hypotheses</em>, not proven results. Here is what would falsify them:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>If acceptance rate does not improve</strong> beyond off-policy training after 1000+ on-policy steps: the exposure bias for draft models may be smaller than for language models (shorter sequences, simpler distribution), making on-policy training unnecessary</li>
  <li><strong>If REINFORCE variance dominates</strong> and training diverges: the binary accept/reject signal may be too sparse. Would need variance reduction (baselines, importance sampling) or switching to a softer acceptance probability estimate</li>
  <li><strong>If disaggregation overhead exceeds the gain</strong>: the communication cost of passing (prompt, draft_tokens) to verify workers and back may dominate for small draft lengths K, making it only worthwhile for K ≥ 3</li>
</ul>

