<!-- ──────────────────────────── §06 RESEARCH AGENDA -->
<section id="s-agenda" data-code="agenda">
<div class="section-no">06 — Research Agenda</div>
<h2>What experiments would settle the key questions</h2>

<h3>Experiment 1: Does on-policy objective matter? (cheapest to run)</h3>
<p>
  Take a trained EAGLE-3 draft model (e.g., for Llama-3-8B). Run 5K additional training steps with: (a) standard off-policy KD on new data, (b) on-policy KD with KL loss only at draft-generated positions, (c) on-policy KD + rejection penalty. Measure acceptance rate on a held-out distribution at K=3 and K=5. Expected: (b) beats (a), (c) beats (b) for K ≥ 3.
</p>

<h3>Experiment 2: Does disaggregation scale? (infrastructure experiment)</h3>
<p>
  Fix the training data pipeline to match a single-server baseline. Add N rollout workers drawing from the same training stream, with weight hot-swaps every M batches. Measure: samples/second, acceptance rate at convergence, wall-clock time to reach target acceptance rate. Expected: linear scaling with N rollout workers up to the verify throughput bottleneck.
</p>

<h3>Experiment 3: Does the PD-disaggregation insight hold?</h3>
<p>
  Route verify requests to the prefill pool of an SGLang deployment. Measure: verify request latency, impact on production prefill latency (should be minimal if batched with regular prefill), utilization of verify GPUs. Expected: verify requests are indistinguishable from prompt prefill in GPU utilization profile.
</p>

<h3>Connection to the FastGRPO / DAS setting</h3>
<p>
  The most interesting deployment context is not standalone spec decoding training but <strong>on-policy RL post-training of the target model</strong> (e.g., GRPO for reasoning). <strong>FastGRPO</strong> (arXiv:2509.21792) already shows that the draft model must update alongside the target during RL training, otherwise acceptance rate degrades from ~0.85 to near 0. <strong>DAS</strong> (arXiv:2511.13841) uses a nonparametric suffix tree instead of a neural draft for the same reason. The RVT architecture would be the correct neural-draft solution for this regime: as the target model's policy distribution shifts during RL training, the rollout/verify/train loop continuously adapts the draft to the updated target.
</p>

<div class="callout-idea">
  <strong>The compound loop:</strong> Outer loop = target model RL training (GRPO/PPO, updating every few hundred steps). Inner loop = draft model RVT training (on-policy, updating every few dozen steps). The draft model lags the target by at most a few dozen training steps rather than being frozen for the entire RL run. This makes spec decoding viable throughout RL post-training, not just at the end.
</div>

<h3>Open questions and risks</h3>
<ul>
  <li><span class="idea-tag open">open</span> <strong>REINFORCE variance</strong>: binary accept/reject signal has high variance. What baseline reduces variance enough to make training stable? TTS's inline update provides a natural baseline (acceptance rate at the previous step). Can importance sampling from Aurora's experience replay stabilise it further?</li>
  <li><span class="idea-tag open">open</span> <strong>Staleness under async weight broadcast</strong>: rollout workers generating with weight version v produce data that trains toward version v+1. Does this staleness bound acceptance rate improvement? verl's analysis suggests staleness of 1-2 gradient steps is negligible for the actor/learner gap — the same analysis likely applies here.</li>
  <li><span class="idea-tag open">open</span> <strong>Draft model capacity</strong>: EAGLE-3 drafts are intentionally tiny (1 transformer layer + LM head). Is on-policy training particularly beneficial for tiny models (where the off-policy distribution mismatch is larger, since the model has less capacity to recover) or does it require a larger draft to represent the accepted-token distribution accurately?</li>
  <li><span class="idea-tag open">open</span> <strong>Objective function choice</strong>: should we minimise KL(π<sub>D</sub> ‖ π<sub>T</sub>) (reverse KL, mode-seeking) or KL(π<sub>T</sub> ‖ π<sub>D</sub>) (forward KL, mode-covering) at accepted positions? GTO's tree reward is agnostic to this. MiniLLM suggests reverse KL for generation quality; but the acceptance rate is directly tied to TV distance which is symmetric. PPOW's window-level reward may be a better target than per-token KL for acceptance rate optimisation.</li>
</ul>
</section>

